{"t":1789920291,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"5b1f90db-b45a-4a24-aa46-62be2fb9da26","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-20T14:40:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 201440 AAA\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion...UPDATED\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n1040 AM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNew Coastal Flood Advisory has been issued for Anne Arundel\nCounty. New Small Craft Advisory has been issued for tonight. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Scattered showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms are \n  expected today. \n\n- 2) Cool and wet conditions continue through the end of the\n  week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms\nare expected today.\n\nOn the synoptic scale, a warm front will be lifting northward \nthrough the forecast area today, with higher dewpoint air \nstreaming in from the south and west. Most guidance does show at\nleast some destabilization today as the deeper moisture works \nin and limited surface heating occurs through a broken cloud \ndeck. Temperatures are forecast to climb into the low to mid 80s\nthis afternoon, with dewpoints reaching into the lower 70s. \nThis yields around 500-1000 J/kg of MLCAPE. Model guidance also \nshows a weak shortwave disturbance moving through around peak \nheating. The best forcing for ascent associated with this \nfeature and the greatest instability are both forecast to be \nacross the southern half of the forecast area. While an \nafternoon thunderstorm can't be ruled out anywhere, the greatest\nchance for a storm appears to be roughly south of I-66 and east\nof the Blue Ridge. However, forecast uncertainty remains high \nwith the activity from WV/OH moving through this morning, which \nisn't being handled particularly well by model guidance. Even if\nstorms do form, the background environment doesn't look \nfavorable for severe storms.\n\nA cold front will move through the area tonight, causing winds\nto shift around to out of the north. Low clouds will build in \novernight, with low temperatures forecast to be in the upper 50s\nto mid 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Cool and wet conditions continue through the\nend of the week.\n\nContinuing northeasterly flow, strong high pressure to our\nnorth, and a developing low pressure system trailing up the \neastern seaboard sets up a classic cold air damming environment \nacross the Mid- Atlantic through much of the week. Temperatures\nremain well- below late-September averages due to persistent \ncloud cover and a stable cool airmass remaining trapped against \nthe Blue Ridge. Several short- wave disturbances tracking across\nthe Mid- Atlantic sustain on and off showers through the end of\nthe week. Substantial cloud cover Thursday and Friday reduce \ninstability and thunderstorm potential. \n\nThis coastal low pressure system offshore continues to move \nnorthward coinciding with a strengthening high pressure system \nlingering in Quebec. Tightening pressure gradients over our \nregion particularly across our Coastal Plains, could lead to \nbreezier winds late Thursday and Friday. Continued onshore flow \ncombined with stronger tidal influence could increase coastal \nflood risk during this period. Refer to the coastal flooding \nsection below for more details. \n\nAs this system tracks to the north of our region by the weekend, \nwindier conditions fade. Shifting northwesterly wind ushers in drier \nconditions and decreasing shower potential to start of the\nweekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /14Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nPrevailing VFR conditions are expected today, although a few\nMVFR clouds may briefly be possible this morning. A major \nforecast question for today is whether storms form again this \nafternoon in the wake of this morning shower activity/cloud \ncover. PROB30 groups for -TSRA have been introduced at many of \nthe terminals this afternoon to account for the potential of \nthunderstorm development, but confidence remains low. Winds \ntoday will start out of the south, before shifting to the \nsouthwest this afternoon. A cold front will move through \ntonight, causing winds to shift around to out of the north. \nCeilings should gradually lower tonight in northerly flow, but \nthere's disagreement in model guidance with respect to just how \nlow ceilings get. Some solutions show MVFR conditions later \ntonight through the day Monday, while others show IFR. For now \nhave MVFR ceilings in the TAFs, but have hinted at the potential\nfor IFR. IFR conditions appear even more likely on Tuesday \nwithin northeasterly flow. Showers will be possible on and off \nthrough both Monday and Tuesday.\n\nIncreasing low and mid-level clouds and potential off and on showers \ncould lead to sub-VFR conditions through the end of the week due to \nonshore flow. Gusty winds remain a possibility mid to late next\nweek with winds continuing to flow out of the north and \nnortheast before the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSmall Craft Advisories continue for the Bay and Lower Tidal\nPotomac within southerly flow through 1 PM. Winds decrease\nslightly and shift to out of the southwest this afternoon. A\ncold front moves over the waters tonight, with winds shifting to\nout of the north. Winds shift to out of the northeast and \ngradually pick up in magnitude for Monday and Tuesday. SCA \nconditions appear likely Monday into Tuesday, with gusts \npotentially nearing 30 knots by Tuesday afternoon.\n\nNorth to northeasterly wind gusts remain elevated through Thursday \nas an area of low pressure travels along the East Coast, with \nshowers possible over the waters during this period. As this \nsystem begins to track past our coastline late in the week, \nwinds could decrease heading into the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\n\nNew Coastal Flood Advisory has been issued for Anne Arundel\nCounty for tonight. Tidal anomalies will increase again within \nonshore flow for Tuesday and Wednesday. With flow more out of \nthe northeast than east, anomalies should be higher the further \nsouth you go in the Bay. At least Minor flooding appears likely \nat some locations on Tuesday and Wednesday. Moderate flooding \nlooks less likely, but can't be completely ruled out at this \npoint.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory until 1 PM EDT this afternoon for MDZ008.\n     Coastal Flood Advisory until 6 AM EDT Monday for MDZ014.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 1 PM EDT this afternoon for \n     ANZ530>534-537>543.\n     Small Craft Advisory from 6 AM to noon EDT Monday for \n     ANZ531>534-539>541-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/KJP/KVH\nAVIATION...LFR/KJP/KVH\nMARINE...LFR/KJP/KVH\n"},"forecast":{"t":1789920239,"days":{"2026-09-20":{"hi":84,"lo":65,"pop":60,"short":"Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-21":{"hi":75,"lo":61,"pop":75,"short":"Mostly Cloudy then Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-22":{"hi":67,"lo":57,"pop":75,"short":"Rain Showers"},"2026-09-23":{"hi":65,"lo":56,"pop":57,"short":"Rain Showers Likely"},"2026-09-24":{"hi":65,"lo":54,"pop":44,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-25":{"hi":67,"lo":54,"pop":21,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-26":{"hi":69,"lo":55,"pop":10,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1789920000,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.3,8.4,7.9,8.3,9.2,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.8,9.8,9.8,9.7,9.3,8.7,5.0,5.0,10.0,10.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,3.0,3.0,3.0,3.0],"spd":[11,11,10,9,8,7,6,5,4,3,3,3,4,5,6,7,7,8,8,9,9,10,11,11,11,10,10,10,11,